</div>}


:: The Locomotive {"position": "1425,1875", "size": "100,100"}
The cab is all firelight and noise. The conductor stands at the open firebox with his coat off and his sleeves rolled up, feeding it.

Not with coal. With tickets.

He looks up. He does not seem surprised.

(if: $trustsConductor)[“$name,” he says, and there is something like relief in it. “I hoped you’d come up front.”

]\
(else:)[“You again,” he says. “You never did listen.”

]\
(if: $ateFeast)[He looks at you more closely, then sighs. “You ate. That makes it harder. The hungry never quite want to get off.”

]\
<span class="examine">(link: "Look into the firebox")[(unless: $clues contains "firebox")[(set: $clues to it + (a: "firebox"))](rerun: ?data)<span class="examined">Thousands of tickets curl and blacken in the flames. Each has been punched once. As they burn, you catch the names: none of them twice, none of them ever coming back.</span>]</span>

<span class="examine">(link: "Ask him who he really is")[<span class="examined">“I was the conductor on the last run out of Ashgrove,” he says. “Eleven passengers and a boy. We went into the fog and we never came out the other side.”

(if: $clues contains "newspaper")[You tell him about the newspaper. October 28, 1891. He nods slowly. “The papers said we disappeared. We didn’t. We just never arrived.”

]\
“Somebody has to punch the tickets,” he says. “Somebody has to keep the fire going for the ones who aren’t ready yet. The man before me did it, and the one before him.” He holds up the ticket punch. It is worn smooth with use. “I’ve been ready for a long while now.”</span>]</span>

<span class="examine">(link: "Look at your ticket")[<span class="examined">You take the ticket out of your pocket. The ink has changed. Where it said //Line 0//, it now gives a destination: <span class="ticket-text">HOME</span>.

There is already one hole punched in it, very small, near the edge. You don’t remember when that happened. You don’t remember a lot of things.</span>]</span>

(if: $thomasFollowing)[Thomas is staring at the conductor. “I know you,” he says. “You told me to sit down and wait.” The conductor lowers his eyes. “I’m sorry, Tommy. I didn’t know what else to say.”

]\
(if: $maraFollowing)[Mara stands at your shoulder, the photograph held against her chest.

]\
“Every passenger gets off eventually,” the conductor says. “One way or another. Give me your ticket and I’ll punch it, and this train will stop where you need it to stop. Or don’t, and ride on. Your choice. It always was.”

{<div class="choices">
(if: $clues's length >= 4 and $sanity >= 25)[(link: "Hand the conductor your ticket")[(if: $maraFollowing and $maraTrust >= 3 and $maraKnows)[(go-to: "Together")](else:)[(go-to: "Home")]]]
(else-if: $sanity < 25)[<span class="locked">Your hands are shaking too badly to let go of the ticket.</span>]
(else:)[<span class="locked">Your fingers will not open around the ticket. You do not yet understand what you would be letting go of.</span>]
(link: "Take the ticket punch from his hand")[(go-to: "The New Conductor")]
(link: "Return to your seat and wait for the final stop")[(go-to: "The Long Sleep")]
</div>}


:: Home [ending end-home] {"position": "200,2050", "size": "100,100"}
You hold out the ticket.

The conductor takes it gently, the way you might take something from a sleeping child. The punch clicks.

The roar of the fire softens. The wheels slow. Through the cab window the fog thins, and thins, and there are lights out there. Not station lamps. Ordinary lights. A street, wet with rain. A row of houses.

On one porch, a light has been left on.

(if: $maraLost)[You think of Mara, somewhere out in the fog, still calling a name she cannot remember. You hope that someone is calling hers.

]\
(else-if: $maraSlept)[Somewhere behind you, in a curtained berth, Mara sleeps on. Perhaps one night she will wake.

]\
(else-if: $maraFollowing)[Mara stays on the step behind you, the photograph in her hand. “Not yet,” she says softly. “I still don’t know what I’m letting go of.” She does not let go of the handrail.

]\
(else:)[Far back along the train, in Seat 13, a woman is still crying. You hope that someone else will sit beside her.

]\
(if: $thomasFollowing)[Thomas hops down onto the platform beside you. His watch has started ticking forward.

]\
The train stops. The door opens. The air smells like rain and cut grass and somebody’s supper.

You step down.

When you look back, there is no train. Only fog, and the sound of iron wheels, very far away, carrying someone else home.

{<div class="ending-card"><span class="ending-num">Ending I of V</span><span class="ending-title">Home</span></div>
<div class="gallery-slot"></div>
<div class="choices">(link: "Return to the station (title screen)")[(restart:)]</div>}


:: Together [ending end-together] {"position": "375,2050", "size": "100,100"}
You hold out the ticket. Beside you, Mara holds out hers. You didn’t know she had one. Perhaps she didn’t either.

The conductor punches them both. Click. Click.

The fire sinks to embers. The wheels slow. The fog outside the cab window thins until you can see a station platform, an ordinary one, wet with rain, with a clock that is running forward.

There is a woman on the platform. She is not young. She wears a faded yellow scarf and holds a black umbrella, and she is looking at the train the way someone looks at a door they have waited at for a very long time.

Mara makes a small sound.

“I never did pick her up,” she says. “But she got home. Somebody got her home.”

(if: $thomasFollowing)[Thomas squeezes past you and jumps down first. His watch is ticking forward. He does not look back.

]\
The door opens. Mara steps down. The woman in the yellow scarf lowers her umbrella, and for a moment the two of them stand in the rain, and neither of them says anything at all.

You step down after them.

Behind you, the Midnight Express pulls away into the fog. In your hand, the photograph has become just a photograph: a little girl in a yellow coat, in front of a house, smiling.

{<div class="ending-card"><span class="ending-num">Ending II of V</span><span class="ending-title">Together</span></div>
<div class="gallery-slot"></div>
<div class="choices">(link: "Return to the station (title screen)")[(restart:)]</div>}


:: The New Conductor [ending end-conductor] {"position": "550,2050", "size": "100,100"}
You reach for the ticket punch.

The conductor lets you take it. His shoulders drop, as though he has set down something very heavy.

(if: $trustsConductor)[“Thank you,” he says. “Look after them.”

]\
(else:)[“There it is,” he says, and smiles for the first time. “You never listen. You’ll do fine.”

]\
He puts on his coat, buttons it, and walks back through the train without another word. You don’t see where he gets off.

The uniform is hanging on a hook by the firebox. It fits you perfectly. It always would have.

(if: $thomasFollowing)[Thomas sits on the coal box and swings his legs. “I’ll stay too,” he says. “Someone should keep you company.”

]\
(if: $maraFollowing)[Mara looks at you for a long moment, then goes back to Seat 13 to wait. You will check on her every night.

]\
The next night, the train stops at a fog-bound platform. A passenger climbs aboard, holding a ticket, looking frightened.

You walk down the aisle to meet them.

“Ticket, please,” you rasp. “You barely made the last run.”

{<div class="ending-card"><span class="ending-num">Ending III of V</span><span class="ending-title">The New Conductor</span></div>
<div class="gallery-slot"></div>
<div class="choices">(link: "Return to the station (title screen)")[(restart:)]</div>}


:: The Long Sleep [ending end-sleep] {"position": "725,2050", "size": "100,100"}
(if: (history:) contains "The Locomotive")[You walk back through the train to your seat. The conductor told you once to stay there until the final stop. You will do as you were told.

The sleeper car is warm. A berth is waiting with the curtain drawn back.]
(else:)[You tell yourself it will only be for a moment.]

You lie down. The pillow is cool. The breathing all around you is slow and even, and after a while, it is your breathing too.

You dream that you are standing on the platform at Ashgrove, holding a ticket, watching a train pull away. You dream it again. And again.

Before you go under completely, you reach up in the dark and, with a fingernail, scratch letters into the wooden frame of the berth. You don’t know why. It seems important that someone should read them.

<span class="sign">DON’T FALL ASLEEP</span>

Somewhere far away, a new passenger sits down in Carriage One, glances at the armrest, and shivers.

{<div class="ending-card"><span class="ending-num">Ending IV of V</span><span class="ending-title">The Long Sleep</span></div>
<div class="gallery-slot"></div>
<div class="choices">(link: "Return to the station (title screen)")[(restart:)]</div>}


:: Nowhere Station [ending end-nowhere flicker] {"position": "900,2050", "size": "100,100"}
(if: $answeredVoice)[“I’m here!” you call. “I’m coming!”

The voice stops.

The fog rushes in over the railing like water into a sinking ship. You do not remember stepping off the train. But you are standing on a platform. The boards are wet. The lamps are rusted on their posts.]
(else:)[The fog gets in.

It comes through the cracks in the windows and under the doors. It fills the carriage until you cannot see your own hands. You try to remember your name, and it takes a long time, and when it comes back it doesn’t sound like yours.

When the fog clears, you are standing on a platform. The boards are wet. The lamps are rusted on their posts.]

A sign hangs from one chain: <span class="sign">ASHGROVE — CLOSED</span>

Behind you, iron wheels groan, and the Midnight Express pulls away into the night without you.

You wait. You are good at waiting. Nights pass. Perhaps years.

Sometimes a train comes through. Its windows are lit. Faces look out at you. And sometimes, when you can’t stand it any longer, you call out to one of them by name.

